---
.title = "Multi-home peers",
.description = "One program reachable over several transports, under one durable name. The id locator names a peer by key and carries its routes as hints, so the routes can change without the peer changing.",
.date = .date("2026-08-24T00:00:00"),
.layout = "page.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

An OCapN locator names one transport and one designator, so a program listening
on two transports is two peers as far as the wire can say. A sturdyref written
against one of them is welded to that path. glowcap's multi-home prototype
closes that gap: the `id` locator names a peer by a durable public key, and the
paths that reach it ride underneath as hints. Use this page when one program
should be reachable over more than one transport without giving every sturdyref
a new address. The feature works in glowcap today, but the on-the-wire names
are still glowcap's private trial profile (`glowcap-next`) until OCapN
standardizes them. The standing section at the end says exactly what that means.

# One peer, several routes

The `id` locator puts the peer's durable public key in the designator, where
every other keyed transport puts its own name. The transport symbol is `id`,
and it is never a byte path of its own: it is a bundle of routes. Dialing it
walks those routes in order, and what makes the answerer *the peer* is never
the route: it is the channel proving the key the locator names.

[Dialing one multi-home peer: the first route is down, so the dial moves on, and the next route's channel proves the key. That proof is what makes whoever answered the peer. The weak fallback can carry a signed gift, never the identity.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-multihome-dial.svg'))

Written out, the routes ride in two hints on one `ocapn://` URI. Each route is
itself a locator, percent-encoded inside the hint and separated by `|`; the
form below is decoded for reading:

```
ocapn://<durable key>.id?via=<route>|<route>&alt-locators=<route>
```

The `via` hint carries the routes that count. A route qualifies only when its
channel can prove a durable key by itself: [iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh'))
authenticates its node id in the QUIC handshake, the
[Noise paths]($link.page('transports/noise')) prove the dialed key while
sealing the stream, and [WebRTC]($link.page('transports/webrtc')) binds the
peer through its signaling authority. A cleartext keyed symbol does not
qualify, because its key is only a dial target.

The `alt-locators` hint carries weak fallbacks, such as a plain socket or an
[onion]($link.page('transports/onion')) address. A weak route can complete the
one handoff its signed gift authorized, and nothing more: it never establishes
the outer identity, never enters the identity's cache, and a sturdyref never
dials it.

Each list is bounded to eight routes, the two together to a few kilobytes,
and every `via` route must name the same key as the designator, so a hostile
locator cannot buy unbounded work or smuggle in a second identity.

# What the other peer sees

Route vocabulary is a negotiated feature named `multi_location`, offered under
glowcap's own `glowcap-next` profile and stamped into both signed hellos before
either side may use it. A first hello never carries it. The floor is per
session: one process can speak route vocabulary with another glowcap host while
its session with a [Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/) vat stays on
the standard profile.

[A negotiated partner receives the id bundle intact. A baseline partner gets one concrete route it can dial, with the private vocabulary stripped, and when no such route exists the give is refused before anything is sent.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-multihome-lowering.svg'))

A handoff can cross those two worlds, because a signed give travels from an
exporter session to a recipient session. The give keeps its route vocabulary
only when both sessions negotiated it. A baseline recipient is handed one
concrete route on a transport it demonstrably participates in, with the
private vocabulary stripped; when no such route exists, the give is refused
before anything is sent. The fallback is never to serialize experimental
vocabulary and hope the other side ignores it, so the Goblins-facing wire is
unchanged with the prototype present.

# Sturdyrefs that survive a transport

The payoff for durable references: a sturdyref written against an `id`
locator names the peer's key, so the peer can move between transports, or
bring a second listener up, without every stored reference going stale.

Redeeming one is gated the same way. A Host that opted into `multi_location`
first reuses any session whose channel already authenticated that key, on any
transport. Otherwise it stages the concrete `via` routes and races the first
two in publisher order, refilling that window from the list as attempts fail;
the first channel that proves the key carries the fetch, and the losing dials
are cancelled. Only after every strong route fails does it try the weak
fallbacks, one at a time in their declared order. The
[multi-home route diagrams](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/08-multihome-routes.md)
draw each of these flows one at a time.

# Rooms runs it

Directory peering in the [rooms app]($link.page('examples')) is the live
consumer. A server started with `--peering` can list a durable room in another
directory through one identity route: its key-proving iroh and Noise listeners
ride under `via`, while onion and plain-socket listeners stay behind as weak
fallbacks. A reader joins the room over whichever path
that reader can open, and the room link does not choose a transport for every
future reader. The foreign listing belongs to the publisher's destination
session; revoke or disconnect removes discovery, while a room already handed
to a reader stays live.

# Standing

Multi-homing is something the OCapN community has wanted for years and not yet
specified; the meeting record reaches from multi-netlayer locators to a
transport-agnostic vat id. glowcap builds it now because it already holds the
ingredients: keyed transports on several byte paths, and a sealed channel that
proves a durable key over any of them.

Everything above this section is built and held by the build's checks. That
covers the bundle vocabulary, the ordered dial with its bounded race and
identity discipline, the per-session negotiation and lowering, `id`
sturdyrefs, and the rooms peering suite that exercises them live.

One shape ships. An earlier design carried a second, comparison-only
vocabulary beside this one so their costs could be weighed; the comparison is
[settled](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0125-identity-route-multihome.md),
and only the `id` locator described above remains. The alternative supplied a
list but no proof that its entries reached one peer, drove no dial, and backed
no sturdyref, so keeping it would have grown the vocabulary without growing
what the product could do. What stays open is the wire vocabulary itself,
which remains a `glowcap-next` extension until OCapN specifies a compatible
shape. Peers that never agree on the extension never see these route hints. A
Goblins peer keeps getting ordinary locators.
[What you can depend on]($link.page('stability')) is the wider evidence
picture behind every claim like that one.
